From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Subject: [PATCH] nss_core: close the N2H netdev lifetime race

nss_core_unregister_subsys_dp() clears subsys_dp_register[if_num] and
returns, and the caller then frees the netdev. Nothing waits for the
receive path, which reads that same ndev from the NAPI poll. The vendor
marked one instance of it themselves:

  /* TODO: Need to ensure the ndev is not removed before we take
   * dev_hold(). */

The hole is wider than that comment. nss_core_handle_virt_if_pkt() at
least takes a dev_hold() on the pointer it read, so only the window
between the read and the hold is exposed. Its siblings do not hold at
all: nss_core_handle_crypto_pkt() and the ext-data path pass ndev
straight into cb()/ext_cb(), the phys-if data path holds only inside its
service-code branch, and around eighteen nss_*_msg_handler() functions
across the subsystem files read the same field into ncm->app_data and
hand it to their registered callback. Fixing only the site carrying the
comment would have left every one of those still exposed.

So do it once, on the side that has exactly one writer. Clearing the
field already stops a later poll from finding the netdev; add the
barrier that waits out the poll which read it before the clear, and the
caller's free is safe for every reader without any reader changing.
softirq is an RCU read-side critical section on non-preemptible kernels
- CONFIG_PREEMPT_NONE with TREE_RCU is what this target builds - which
is what makes synchronize_net() sufficient here. On a CONFIG_PREEMPT_RCU
kernel the readers would need an explicit rcu_read_lock() as well.

This makes nss_core_unregister_subsys_dp() a sleeping function, and the
kernel will not say so: OpenWrt ships CONFIG_DEBUG_ATOMIC_SLEEP unset, so
might_sleep() compiles to nothing and an atomic caller sleeps silently
rather than warning. The contract is therefore carried by inspection.

Of the 51 call sites, 49 unregister from process context on netdev
teardown and are unaffected. Two hold a spinlock across it, and both are
fixed here: nss_rmnet_rx_destroy_sync() and nss_virt_if_destroy_sync(),
which are the same function under two names. Each read the two
registrations, cleared them and dropped the device reference inside
spin_lock_bh(&nss_top_main.lock); with the barrier added above, that
sleeps with bottom halves disabled.

Drop the lock in both rather than hoisting the calls out of it, because
it protects nothing. nss_top_main.lock otherwise covers only the
shaper-bounce registrant and nss_ctx->state; these two functions are its
only users that touch subsys_dp_register at all. The registration side
writes that array unlocked, and every receive-path reader reads ndev from
it bare. Narrowing the region would keep two lines asserting an exclusion
that does not exist, which is what admitted the sleeping call in the
first place.

Nothing that ran before could have relied on the lock either:
nss_dynamic_interface_dealloc_node(), reached a few lines below the
unlock in both functions, blocks on wait_for_completion_timeout()
unconditionally, so no caller has ever been able to hold a lock across
these functions.

The rmnet_rx arm reproduces on an IPQ8074 with NSS core 1 running and
NSS_DRV_RMNET_ENABLE set: bringing a bound raw-IP interface down gives
"BUG: scheduling while atomic" with preempt_count 0x200 at
synchronize_net() under nss_rmnet_rx_destroy_sync(), then a second at
0xfffffe00 - the underflow left by the first - and the machine stops
responding. The virt_if arm is the same defect but was not reproduced:
its two callers are reachable only with the mac80211 nss_redirect module
parameter set, which nothing in this tree sets, or when a shaped device
has no firmware representation, which does not arise while the bridge
manager is built.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>

--- a/nss_core.c
+++ b/nss_core.c
@@ -389,6 +389,18 @@ void nss_core_unregister_subsys_dp(struc
 	reg->ndev = NULL;
 	reg->features = 0;
 	reg->type = 0;
+
+	/*
+	 * The N2H receive path reads reg->ndev in the NAPI poll and hands it to
+	 * a callback, or holds it - "TODO: Need to ensure the ndev is not
+	 * removed before we take dev_hold()" in nss_core_handle_virt_if_pkt().
+	 * Clearing the field stops any later poll from finding it; this waits
+	 * out the poll that already read it, so the caller can free the netdev
+	 * once we return. Softirq is an RCU read-side section on the
+	 * non-preemptible kernels this builds for, which is what makes one
+	 * barrier here cover every reader instead of a hold at each one.
+	 */
+	synchronize_net();
 }
 
 /*
--- a/nss_rmnet_rx.c
+++ b/nss_rmnet_rx.c
@@ -388,9 +388,7 @@ nss_tx_status_t nss_rmnet_rx_destroy_syn
 		return NSS_TX_FAILURE_NOT_READY;
 	}
 
-	spin_lock_bh(&nss_top_main.lock);
 	if (!nss_ctx->subsys_dp_register[if_num_n2h].ndev || !nss_ctx->subsys_dp_register[if_num_h2n].ndev) {
-		spin_unlock_bh(&nss_top_main.lock);
 		nss_warning("%px: Unregister virt interface %d %d: no context\n", nss_ctx, if_num_n2h, if_num_h2n);
 		return NSS_TX_FAILURE_BAD_PARAM;
 	}
@@ -399,7 +397,6 @@ nss_tx_status_t nss_rmnet_rx_destroy_syn
 	nss_assert(dev == nss_ctx->subsys_dp_register[if_num_h2n].ndev);
 	nss_core_unregister_subsys_dp(nss_ctx, if_num_n2h);
 	nss_core_unregister_subsys_dp(nss_ctx, if_num_h2n);
-	spin_unlock_bh(&nss_top_main.lock);
 	dev_put(dev);
 
 	status = nss_rmnet_rx_handle_destroy_sync(handle);
--- a/nss_virt_if.c
+++ b/nss_virt_if.c
@@ -489,9 +489,7 @@ nss_tx_status_t nss_virt_if_destroy_sync
 		return NSS_TX_FAILURE_NOT_READY;
 	}
 
-	spin_lock_bh(&nss_top_main.lock);
 	if (!nss_ctx->subsys_dp_register[if_num_n2h].ndev || !nss_ctx->subsys_dp_register[if_num_h2n].ndev) {
-		spin_unlock_bh(&nss_top_main.lock);
 		nss_warning("%px: Unregister virt interface %d %d: no context\n", nss_ctx, if_num_n2h, if_num_h2n);
 		return NSS_TX_FAILURE_BAD_PARAM;
 	}
@@ -500,7 +498,6 @@ nss_tx_status_t nss_virt_if_destroy_sync
 	nss_assert(dev == nss_ctx->subsys_dp_register[if_num_h2n].ndev);
 	nss_core_unregister_subsys_dp(nss_ctx, if_num_n2h);
 	nss_core_unregister_subsys_dp(nss_ctx, if_num_h2n);
-	spin_unlock_bh(&nss_top_main.lock);
 	dev_put(dev);
 
 	status = nss_virt_if_handle_destroy_sync(handle);
